-- title:
--   Pushouts of compactly generated weakly Hausdorff spaces along closed embeddings are computed in $\mathbf{Top}$
-- statement:
--   The pushout, in the category of all topological spaces, of compactly generated weakly Hausdorff spaces along a closed embedding is compactly generated weakly Hausdorff.
--
--   A topological space is *compactly generated* if its topology is determined by the continuous maps into it from compact Hausdorff spaces (no separation axiom), and *weakly Hausdorff* if the image of every continuous map from a compact Hausdorff space is closed. Let $\mathbf{Top}$ be the category of topological spaces and $\mathbf{CGWH}$ its full subcategory of compactly generated weakly Hausdorff spaces.
--
--   Let $X$ and $Z$ be compactly generated weakly Hausdorff spaces, let $P$ be a topological space, let $p:P\to Z$ be a closed embedding and $q:P\to X$ a continuous map, and let
--   $$\begin{array}{ccc}P&\xrightarrow{\ p\ }&Z\\ {\scriptstyle q}\downarrow&&\downarrow{\scriptstyle g}\\ X&\xrightarrow{\ f\ }&Y\end{array}$$
--   be a pushout square in $\mathbf{Top}$; thus $Y=X\sqcup_PZ$ is the quotient of the disjoint union $X\sqcup Z$ by the equivalence relation generated by $q(a)\sim p(a)$, $a\in P$.
--
--   **Statement.**
--
--   1. $Y$ is compactly generated and weakly Hausdorff. Consequently the square is also a pushout square in $\mathbf{CGWH}$: the pushout in $\mathbf{CGWH}$ "can be computed in $\mathbf{Top}$".
--   2. $f:X\to Y$ is a closed embedding.
--   3. The square is cartesian in $\mathbf{Top}$: the natural map $P\to X\times_{f,Y,g}Z=\{(x,z)\in X\times_0Z: f(x)=g(z)\}$ is a homeomorphism (so this fibre product is compactly generated weakly Hausdorff and is the fibre product in $\mathbf{CGWH}$).
--
--   General colimits in $\mathbf{CGWH}$ are not computed in $\mathbf{Top}$ (one has to apply the weak Hausdorffification), and the corresponding statement fails for compactly generated *Hausdorff* spaces: collapsing a closed subset of a non-regular Hausdorff space gives a non-Hausdorff pushout. This permanence property is therefore one of the main reasons for preferring the weak Hausdorff condition; it covers cell attachments and quotients $Z/P$ by closed subspaces. The paper uses it to identify the regular monomorphisms and the epimorphisms of $\mathbf{CGWH}$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The spaces are objects of Mathlib's `TopCat.{u}`, `IsPushout q p f g` and `IsPullback q p f g` are the categorical notions in `TopCat`, and `IsClosedEmbedding` is applied to the underlying functions. `CompactlyGeneratedSpace` is Mathlib's notion (test spaces `CompHaus.{u}`) and `CGWH.WeaklyHausdorffSpace` is from the definition file `CGWH_WeakHausdorff`. **Deviation from the source.** The source takes $Y$ to be the pushout formed in $\mathbf{CGWH}$ and asserts that it "can be computed in $\mathbf{Spaces}$"; here the same content is stated starting from the pushout in $\mathbf{Top}$. The source also assumes that $P$ is compactly generated weakly Hausdorff; this is automatic, because $P$ is homeomorphic to a closed subspace of $Z$, and is not assumed.
-- source:
--   A. M. Masullo, The Music of the Primes, draft of 20 November 2026, https://math216.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/the-music-of-the-primes.pdf, pp. 859-861, Lemma E.2.3.10

import Definitions.Def_CGWH_WeakHausdorff

open CategoryTheory Topology TopologicalSpace

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace CGWH

theorem weaklyHausdorffSpace_of_isPushout_of_isClosedEmbedding
    {P X Z Y : TopCat.{u}} {q : P ⟶ X} {p : P ⟶ Z} {f : X ⟶ Y} {g : Z ⟶ Y}
    [CompactlyGeneratedSpace X] [WeaklyHausdorffSpace X]
    [CompactlyGeneratedSpace Z] [WeaklyHausdorffSpace Z]
    (sq : IsPushout q p f g) (hp : IsClosedEmbedding p) :
    CompactlyGeneratedSpace Y ∧ WeaklyHausdorffSpace Y ∧ IsClosedEmbedding f ∧
      IsPullback q p f g := by sorry

end CGWH
